The Security Council,

Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, and the primary responsibility of the Security Council under the Charter for the maintenance of international peace and security,

Expressing grave concern over the acts of sabotage committed against the Nord Stream 1 and Nord Stream 2 undersea gas pipeline systems in the Baltic Sea in September 2022, which caused severe damage to critical transnational energy infrastructure and significant methane gas leakages into the marine environment,

Stressing that the destruction of critical transnational energy infrastructure poses a grave threat to international peace and security, economic stability, energy security, and the global marine environment,

Recognizing the urgent need to ensure full accountability and prevent impunity for attacks against critical international civilian infrastructure,

Emphasizing that a comprehensive, transparent, impartial, objective, and professional investigation into all aspects of the acts of sabotage against the Nord Stream pipelines is essential to establish the truth, identify the perpetrators, sponsors, organizers, and accomplices, and bring them to justice,

Taking note of the national investigations launched by certain coastal States, and stressing the importance of timely, transparent, and collaborative efforts to provide the international community with definitive and credible conclusions,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the acts of sabotage perpetrated against the Nord Stream 1 and Nord Stream 2 gas pipelines;

2. Requests the Secretary-General of the United Nations to establish an independent international commission of inquiry to conduct a comprehensive, transparent, and impartial international investigation into all aspects of the acts of sabotage against the Nord Stream 1 and Nord Stream 2 pipelines, including the identification of the perpetrators, organizers, sponsors, and accomplices;

3. Calls upon the Secretary-General to appoint qualified, experienced, and impartial international experts to the commission of inquiry, taking into account balanced geographical representation;

4. Calls upon all relevant States, including those currently carrying out national investigations, to cooperate fully and share relevant information, findings, and evidence with the international commission of inquiry in a timely manner;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the proposed modalities, terms of reference, and composition of the commission of inquiry within thirty days of the adoption of this resolution;

6. Further requests the commission of inquiry to submit a final report containing its findings and recommendations to the Security Council upon the conclusion of its work;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

